{"id":"noise-prediction-loss","t":"Noise-prediction loss for a toy denoiser","tags":["Diffusion Models","Loss Functions"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"eps_loss","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"The standard DDPM objective is plain regression: noise a clean example to a random step, ask the network which noise was added, and score it with mean squared error.\n\n$$x_t = \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t}\\,x_0 + \\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}\\,\\varepsilon, \\qquad \\mathcal L = \\operatorname{mean}\\big(\\lVert \\hat\\varepsilon_\\theta(x_t, t) - \\varepsilon \\rVert^2\\big)$$\n\nThe \"network\" here is a toy linear model with a learned per-step bias, so you can focus on the objective:\n\n$$\\hat\\varepsilon_\\theta(x_t, t) = x_t\\,W + E_t$$\n\nwhere $E_t$ is row $t$ of a bias table $E$.\n\nWrite `eps_loss(x0, t, noise, alpha_bars, W, E)`.\n\n## Inputs\n- `x0`: `(B, D)` clean data; `noise`: `(B, D)` the $\\varepsilon$ to add; `t`: `(B,)` integer steps (0-indexed).\n- `alpha_bars`: `(T,)`; `W`: `(D, D)`, applied as `x_t @ W`; `E`: `(T, D)`.\n\n## Output\nA Python `float`: the mean of the squared error over **all** $B \\times D$ entries.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef eps_loss(x0, t, noise, alpha_bars, W, E):\n    \"\"\"Noise x0 to step t, predict eps = x_t @ W + E[t], return the mean squared error (float).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Two stages: build `x_t` with the forward-diffusion formula, then run the model on it. The target is the **noise**, not `x0`.","`alpha_bars[t][:, None]` gives a `(B, 1)` column so each row gets its own noise level. `E[t]` gives a `(B, D)` array of bias rows.","`float(np.mean((x_t @ W + E[t] - noise) ** 2))`."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef eps_loss(x0, t, noise, alpha_bars, W, E):\n    x0, noise, W, E = (np.asarray(a, dtype=float) for a in (x0, noise, W, E))\n    ab = np.asarray(alpha_bars, dtype=float)[t][:, None]     # (B, 1): one noise level per row\n    x_t = np.sqrt(ab) * x0 + np.sqrt(1.0 - ab) * noise\n    pred = x_t @ W + E[t]                                    # E[t] gathers one bias row per example\n    return float(np.mean((pred - noise) ** 2))\n","ex_md":"## Why predicting noise is enough\nThe DDPM variational bound breaks into per-step KL terms between Gaussians. If the reverse mean is written as $\\mu_\\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}}\\big(x_t - \\frac{\\beta_t}{\\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}}\\hat\\varepsilon_\\theta\\big)$, each KL becomes a weighted squared error on $\\hat\\varepsilon$. Dropping the weights gives this \"simple\" loss, which trains better in practice because it puts relatively more weight on the hard, noisy steps.\n\nTest 2 is the sanity check: with $\\bar\\alpha = 0$, $x_t = \\varepsilon$ exactly, and an identity model is perfect (loss $0$). Test 5 runs the same identity model at mixed noise levels, where it is wrong by $\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t}x_0 + (\\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alpha_t} - 1)\\varepsilon$.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(BD^2)$ for the matrix product; the rest is $O(BD)$.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Regressing onto `x0` or `x_t` instead of the noise.\n- Using one $t$ for the whole batch, or broadcasting `alpha_bars[t]` of shape `(B,)` against `(B, D)` without a trailing axis.\n- Summing over $D$ and averaging over $B$: a different scale from the mean over all entries (it changes the effective learning rate).\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Why does the toy model need the bias table $E$ at all? What would a model with no access to $t$ learn?\n- What changes if you regress on $x_0$ or $v$ instead? (See the parameterization and Min-SNR problems.)","tests":[{"a":[{"__nd__":[[1.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0],"dtype":"int64"},{"__nd__":[[0.0,1.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0.25],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[0.0,0.0],[0.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[0.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"}],"e":0.5},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[2.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[1],"dtype":"int64"},{"__nd__":[[1.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0.5,0.0],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[1.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[0.0],[0.0]],"dtype":"float64"}],"e":0.0},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[0.0,0.0],[0.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0,1],"dtype":"int64"},{"__nd__":[[1.0,-1.0],[2.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0.0,0.0],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[1.0,0.0],[0.0,1.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[0.0,0.0],[0.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"}],"e":0.0},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[1.8267565599574231,-3.0783319101980338,0.9580639753088469],[0.06963722766094482,1.3182500241810684,0.385629249998389],[1.8272586275861753,0.0317437591517664,-0.5162294444924808],[0.5804849213397179,0.43210686133773885,-0.35683935740335093],[-0.24730382198818454,0.7194406781853278,0.7043159938619936]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0,50,99,50,10],"dtype":"int64"},{"__nd__":[[-0.4939342302351804,-0.3677137240199963,-1.8067903895865323],[1.6792074674884705,-0.2242908783928769,1.337277430495411],[0.4174655938071864,1.9439627746249157,1.537109976128164],[0.318298439352904,1.480763419858435,-0.9501235216099734],[1.2586181429890126,-1.4804236275921896,0.3432363675742628]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0.9999,0.99959902,0.9990972010980405,0.9983948054899957,0.9974922162466487,0.9963899369693014,0.9950885913243506,0.9935889224574254,0.9918917922878542,0.9899981806843956,0.9879091845233353,0.9856260166302147,0.9831500046066194,0.980482589543616,0.9776253246235824,0.9745798736123308,0.9713480092435739,0.9679316114979314,0.9643326657788164,0.9605532609876832,0.9565955875012502,0.9524619350534417,0.9481546905249223,0.9436763356432106,0.9390294445964827,0.9342166815642782,0.9292407981684312,0.9241046308476457,0.9188110981592245,0.9133631980115531,0.9077640048310156,0.9020166666670956,0.8961244022394833,0.8900904979310708,0.8839183047307709,0.8776112351301465,0.8711727599778736,0.864606405296101,0.8579157490627944,0.8511044179641747,0.8441760841213733,0.8371344617954397,0.8299833040748296,0.8227263995495046,0.8153675689757562,0.8079106619358507,0.8003595534965655,0.7927181408706568,0.7849903400852601,0